welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Emma Skye a senior fellow at the  Jackson Institute at the Macmillan  Center where she lectures on Iraq and  Middle East politics she is a British  citizen who has worked at senior levels  on behalf of the US and UK governments  in Iraq Afghanistan and Jerusalem across  the fields of development defense and  diplomacy and with both civilian and  military agencies today we talk with  Professor sky about her new book the  unraveling high hopes and missed  opportunities in Iraq  welcome professor sky thank you let's  begin with an overview of your book tell  us about it the book tells about my  experiences in Iraq I first went through  Iraq in 2003  why was the senior civilian  representative of the coalition in  Kirkuk and it goes through the surge in  2007 when I was a political adviser to  general Odierno then through the  drawdown of US troops and ends in 2014  with the takeover of a third of the  country by the Islamic state I felt and  witnessed so many of these key events  that I felt almost a duty to write about  it and I wanted to honor the efforts of  all those who worked in Iraq and strive  to make the country a better place I  wanted to remember the people who lost  their lives and I wanted to pay tribute  to the country of Iraq it's a country  that I came very much to love so you  have been you were there for more than a  decade and you actually volunteered to  help rebuild Iraq after the fall of the  regime why did you do that well the  British government asked for volunteers  to help rebuild the country it was  supposed to be for three months before  we hand in the country back over to the  Iraqis three months three months it was  supposed to be hmm and I was against the  war and I thought is my chance to go to  Iraq to apologize to Iraqis for the war  to help them rebuild the country I'd  worked for a decade in Israel Palestine  and I felt I had skills to contribute in  conflict mediation and institutional  development okay so in the decade or so  that you were there how would you  categorize your experiences and the time  you spent there it was the most  challenging experience of my life I was  nearly killed a number of times  Wow and people I know lost their lives  there but I grew as a person I made  extraordinary bonds with American  soldiers and I made really great Iraqi  friends tell us about a daily life what  did you do on a daily basis give us some  examples of what it was like for you no  day in Iraq is the same so it's very  difficult to talk about a typical day  because it wasn't a typical day okay but  the morning would start very early  you'll be going to meetings with the US  military where all the events of the  evening the day before were covered then  deciding what was the schedule for the  day and then going out it's often I  would go with general Odierno to visit  troops then we would go and have  meetings with Iraqis right the way  through till 7:00 or 8:00 o'clock and  then at night I would go out and see  more Iraqis were you in the middle of  any kind of gunfire well I'd only been  in Iraq a week when my house was  attacked with me in it so how was it  attack insurgents came to the front door  with rockets mm-hm and the Rockets came  into the house but unfortunately or  fortunately for me they exploded between  the ceiling and the bed where I was  sleeping I was very fortunate that was  in my first week and why did you not run  run run back to England I couldn't now I  couldn't  I really felt I've got to stay and help  rebuild this country had you ever ever  been in anything like that before and in  other countries was that your first  experience with that kind of a situation  well I had worked in Gaza and the West  Bank before so I was used to a difficult  situation but I never experienced  anything like Iraq when I myself could  be a target let alone just in the wrong  place  so you originally you believe that  basically a stable government was on the  brink of happening and then things went  very awry and that opportunity was  squandered what happened you know during  the surge the level of violence in Iraq  decreased dramatically and we and the  Iraqis felt the country's on the right  track it's gonna go in the right  direction  in 2010 the national elections were held  high participation and the results were  very very close very very close  contested results and unfortunately the  US didn't uphold the rights of the  winning bloc to have first go at trying  to form the government instead the  government insisted and said the US  government insisted on keeping the  incumbent Nouri al-maliki in power and  why did they do that do you think I  think because it appeared the easiest  option Obama had campaigned to end the  Iraq war it seemed easier to keep the  incumbent rather than trying to help a  new government be formed with a new  leader and unfortunately Maliki then  went on to follow very sectarian  policies alienated the Sunni population  there were widespread protests and the  Islamic state rose to power so in that  moment when that occurred what was going  on in your head did you know right out  that that would be a terrible decision  yes I did because it meant that people  don't trust the system it's a very new  system people have got to believe that  change comes about through elections  rather through violence if they try  elections and they can't bring about  change then they lose faith in the  system so the people on the ground like  yourself or probably were they not  saying to the US government listen this  is a mistake yes this was a internal  debate and unfortunately those who said  look give the winning block first go it  might not succeed but at least people  could see the elections have been  honored the guy who won the elections  gets first go at trying to form the  government if he fails it goes to the  next candidate okay so unfortunately  then that was the the decline to where  we are today or were their subsequent  mistakes is well made that was the major  mistake because it was very difficult to  recover from that afterwards so today  what you what you think needs to happen  in Iraq for a stable government to  develop I think it's very difficult to  form a stable government in Iraq the  Islamic state needs to be defeated the  sheer militias need to be demobilized  and you've got to push Iran back out of  Iraq and power needs to be decentralized  away from Baghdad so now the province is  more say in their own affairs mm-hmm do  you foresee yourself going back there  and continuing the work that you had  been doing I don't foresee myself going  back to Iraq to work but so I go back on  a regular basis to visit my friends in  Iraq and what is their life like now  given the current situation it depends  it's very different in different parts  of the country in the south where the  majority of the population is shir  there's progress being made people's  standard of living is improving in  Kurdistan there has been good progress  as well but in the Sunni areas of Iraq  this massive population displacement  lots of people have been kicked out of  their homes or fled in fear what do you  think the future holds for Iraq it's  hard to be optimistic Lee speaking it's  hard to be optimistic about the future  of Iraq but it's a land with a great  history it gave the world the written  law  first settled agriculture is where the  Talmud was written Baghdad used to be  capital of culture of the Arab world and  so we have to hope that a new generation  will come that will draw inspiration  from that great history if there's one  thing that you'd like readers to take  from your book a lesson perhaps what  would it be I think the lesson would be  a lesson of common humanity that really  there's much more that unites us than  divides us very good thank you so much  for being here with us today and sharing  your work thank you very much for more  information about Professor sky and her  book please visit our website at  yale.edu slash macmillan report be sure  to join us again for another episode of  the Macmillan report made possible  through funding from the Whitney and  Betty Macmillan Center for International  and area studies at Yale
 

